<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Aksumite Yemen</h1><p class="st-meta">525 CE – 570 CE (approx.) · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yemen under Ethiopian Christian rule, best known for Abraha, who repaired the Marib Dam and, by tradition, marched on Mecca with an elephant.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 525 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaleb">King Kaleb</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to avenge the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massacre-of-najran">Christians of Najran</a>, defeated the Himyarite king Yusuf and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumyafa-ashwa">Sumyafa Ashwa</a>, a local Christian, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>. Within a few years the Aksumite general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a> overthrew him and made himself ruler, nominally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a> governed in practice independently from Sanaa. His inscriptions record the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Marib Dam</a> in 548 and a diplomatic conference attended by envoys of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the Arab kings. He built a great church in Sanaa and campaigned deep into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>; Muslim tradition remembers his failed expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the Year of the Elephant.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha's</a> sons Yaksum and Masruq were unpopular. The Himyarite noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</a> sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and around 570 a Sasanian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahriz">Wahriz</a> defeated the Ethiopians, opening the period of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sasanian-yemen">Sasanian Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 525 CE – 570 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:70.15%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>525 CE – 570 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sumyafa Ashwa, Abraha, Masruq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sabaic, Ge'ez</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Yemen</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Abraha</dd></dl></aside></div>
